## Collaboration service
Use a dedicated Yjs-compatible WebSocket collaboration service for CRDT-based document synchronization. Prefer a dedicated Node.js service using y-websocket or another compatible maintained Yjs server.

The Java Spring Boot application remains the main application backend for authentication, business logic, room permissions, project metadata, snapshots, and persistent storage.

The collaboration service must validate room access using a secure backend-issued authorization mechanism. Never trust an arbitrary room ID supplied by a client.

Do not attempt to implement a custom CRDT from scratch unless necessary.

## Audio/video calling
Use LiveKit for WebRTC-based audio, video, and screen sharing.

The Spring Boot backend must generate short-lived, room-scoped LiveKit access tokens using a compatible server SDK or a correctly implemented, tested signing mechanism.

LiveKit URL and credentials must come from environment variables.

Do not use a fake video-call screen, simulated participant tiles, or randomly generated user avatars as a substitute for a real call.

