## Call authorization
When a user clicks Join Call:
1. Frontend calls an authenticated Spring Boot endpoint.
2. Backend verifies room membership and capacity.
3. Backend issues a short-lived LiveKit token restricted to the correct room and identity.
4. Frontend connects to the configured LiveKit server.
5. Participant appears only after a real successful connection.

Never expose the LiveKit API secret in frontend JavaScript.

Prevent unauthorized users from obtaining call tokens.

Enforce the 6-participant limit through server-side room configuration or an appropriate LiveKit server-side admission mechanism, not just the frontend.

If a user has joined the coding room but not the call, distinguish coding-room presence from active call presence.

## Screen sharing
Use the LiveKit screen-sharing API. Display a clear indication when sharing is active and allow the user to stop sharing.

If screen capture is unsupported or permission is denied, show an understandable error.

Screen sharing and media permissions require a secure context, normally HTTPS or localhost during development.

## Production requirements
Configure:
- LiveKit server URL
- LiveKit API key
- LiveKit API secret
- Appropriate TURN/STUN infrastructure through LiveKit configuration

Use a hosted LiveKit service or a properly configured self-hosted deployment.

Do not claim reliable calling over arbitrary internet networks until the TURN configuration has been tested.

If calling credentials are absent, show a clearly labelled configuration-required state and instructions. Never silently simulate a successful call.

